    "digdeeper": {
      "type": "zeta-accordion",
      "blocks": {
        "a1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Description",
            "content": "<h3>Three molecules. <em>One stack.</em></h3><p>ZETA LONGEVITY is a complete bioflavonoid complex with resveratrol, quercetin, and pterostilbene — three compounds that work synergistically on distinct longevity mechanisms: sirtuin activation, senescent cell clearance, and antioxidant/anti-inflammatory protection.</p><h4>Why These Three Together</h4><p>Pterostilbene is the methylated form of resveratrol — bio-optimized with greater hepatic stability, better absorption, and a longer half-life. Quercetin synergizes with both, clearing the accumulated cellular debris that slows recovery and drives inflammation. This isn't a collection of trending ingredients. It's a stack with a coherent mechanism.</p><p style=\"font-size:11.5px;color:var(--zeta-mid);font-style:italic;margin-top:28px;\">*These statements have not been evaluated by the Food and Drug Administration. This product is not intended to diagnose, treat, cure, or prevent any disease.</p>",
            "open_by_default": true
          }
        },
        "a2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Ingredients",
            "content": "<h3>What's <em>in it.</em></h3><h4>Active Ingredients</h4><ul>\n<li>\n<strong>Quercetin Dihydrate</strong> (from Sophora japonica bud) — 250 mg. Senolytic bioflavonoid. Supports clearance of senescent cells and has a demonstrated anti-inflammatory profile, including RCT-level CRP reduction data.</li>\n<li>\n<strong>trans-Resveratrol</strong> (from Polygonum cuspidatum root extract) — 75 mg. Sirtuin activator. The most-studied polyphenol in longevity research.</li>\n<li>\n<strong>trans-Pterostilbene</strong> (PteroPure® — ChromaDex) — 62.5 mg. Methylated resveratrol analog. ~7× more bioavailable, longer plasma half-life, and superior cellular uptake versus resveratrol alone.</li>\n</ul><h4>Other Ingredients</h4><p>Capsule (hypromellose and water), microcrystalline cellulose, stearic acid, magnesium stearate, silica, medium-chain triglyceride oil. MCT oil aids fat-soluble absorption — no need to take with a fatty meal.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "a3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Directions & Precautions",
            "content": "<h3>How to <em>take it.</em></h3><p>Take 1 capsule twice daily. The formula includes MCT oil — fat-soluble absorption is built in. Take with or without food. Morning and evening is the simplest pattern. Any consistent schedule works.</p><h4>Precautions</h4><p>Pregnant or nursing? Check with your provider. Quercetin can interact with certain antibiotics (especially fluoroquinolones) — worth a conversation with your physician if relevant. If you're on blood thinners, resveratrol at higher doses may have mild antiplatelet effects; discuss with your doctor.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "a4": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Research",
            "content": "<h3>The <em>evidence.</em></h3><div class=\"ref\"><strong>Baur JA, Sinclair DA.</strong> Therapeutic potential of resveratrol: the in vivo evidence. <em>Nature Reviews Drug Discovery</em> (2006). The paper that put resveratrol on the map — sirtuin activation, caloric restriction mimicry, and the longevity pathway case made rigorously.</div><div class=\"ref\"><strong>Hsu YH et al.</strong> Effect of pterostilbene on glucose and lipid-related markers in a randomized controlled trial. <em>Nutrition Journal</em> (2012). Human RCT showing pterostilbene's metabolic effects — one of the cleaner clinical demonstrations in this space.</div><div class=\"ref\"><strong>Riche DM et al.</strong> Analysis of safety from a human clinical trial with pterostilbene. <em>Journal of Toxicology</em> (2013). Establishes the safety profile at doses relevant to supplementation. Pterostilbene was well-tolerated.</div><div class=\"ref\"><strong>Hickson LJ et al.</strong> Senolytics decrease senescent cells in humans: preliminary report from a clinical trial of dasatinib plus quercetin in individuals with diabetic kidney disease. <em>EBioMedicine</em> (2019). First-in-human senolytic trial — the quercetin study that legitimized the senescence clearance angle.</div><div class=\"ref\"><strong>Serban MC et al.</strong> Effects of quercetin on glycaemic control in patients with type 2 diabetes. <em>Nutrition</em> (2016). Pooled RCT analysis demonstrating quercetin's significant reduction in circulating CRP.</div>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        }
      },
